      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785969927324,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we improve pagination consistency and efficiency?\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we improve pagination consistency and efficiency?\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "fMJ_QOSYa-PFGsrSakCz7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 997.0296554485984,
      "y": 387.52936643588396,
      "width": 914.8311767578125,
      "height": 180,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b02",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 99185232,
      "version": 440,
      "versionNonce": 34864208,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785969917481,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- if new article are constantly being published, traditional paged based pagination will not work (pulling latest article)\n- Solution: Monotonically increase article IDs\n-- Instead of using random UUIDs, we can use time-ordered UUIDs (like ULIDs) or database auto-increment IDs,\n that naturally increase with each new article. --> newer articles will always have higher IDs than older ones.\n-- query (cursor base): `WHERE article_id < cursor_id ORDER BY article_id DESC LIMIT 20`\n-- Limitation: require planning the ID strategy upfront, since migration from unordered -> ordered can be complex\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- if new article are constantly being published, traditional paged based pagination will not work (pulling latest article)\n- Solution: Monotonically increase article IDs\n-- Instead of using random UUIDs, we can use time-ordered UUIDs (like ULIDs) or database auto-increment IDs,\n that naturally increase with each new article. --> newer articles will always have higher IDs than older ones.\n-- query (cursor base): `WHERE article_id < cursor_id ORDER BY article_id DESC LIMIT 20`\n-- Limitation: require planning the ID strategy upfront, since migration from unordered -> ordered can be complex\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q_3nF3MzB0Jv6G4anF0fv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 996.175139942588,
      "y": 530.9195111315157,
      "width": 435.8556213378906,
      "height": 40,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
